00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.610
It's about a mile south.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:28.780
This is allowed.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:29.980
Look, you have no...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.800
right to do that. Sorry ma'am. I'd like to see your commanding officer, please. Where

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:41.520
could I find him? You'll find him in Paxton. He headquarters there. I'll leave

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.640
your camera at the roadblock.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.020
I'd like to see your commanding officer, please.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:04.820
Oh, I'm sorry, but the colonel's busy.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.240
Perhaps Captain Barton can help you.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:13.790
Sentry.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.070
Well, this will cover the whole situation you asked for, sir.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:18.810
Thanks, Lieutenant.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:20.830
Sit down, please.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:26.140
Are you the Audrey Ames who covered Korea for that picture magazine?

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:26.820
That's right.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.980
I read the book you wrote after the war.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:29.980
Liked it very much.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:30.840
Well, thank you.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.340
You're with National Wire Service now.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.680
I was on my way to Chanute Field to do a picture story on a new jet plane they're unveiling,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.760
and I ran into a roadblock.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:41.460
So?

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:47.660
Captain, there was a town beyond that roadblock, a town that isn't there anymore.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.350
Until we find out exactly what happened, we'd like to avoid publicity.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.520
You have any idea what happened?

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:55.440
I'm sorry, mister.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.870
Captain, you can't suppress a story like this.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:04.070
We're not trying to, Miss Ames, but until we have more facts...

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.980
Look, will you give us your word?

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.080
You won't release a story until we give the go-ahead.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:10.460
You have my word.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.930
Sometime during the night, the town of Ludlow was completely demolished.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.720
The town's population, about 150 people, vanished.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:21.400
Vanished?

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:22.790
No bodies, nothing.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:25.670
Well, there must be some trace.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.970
I know it's hard to believe, Miss Ames, but a special detail combed through the record.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:38.540
for two solid hours and couldn't find a thing was it an explosion we don't know a hundred and

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:43.700
fifty people just don't vanish into thin air we're still trying to find out what happened if

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:58.220
you'd like to sit in yes thank you i would dave what time did you leave ludlow last night must

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.260
went out at 11 o'clock

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:04.800
My son-in-law watched the television news at 10.30, so I sat there and watched that.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.040
And my daughter wanted me to sit there and talk a while.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.250
Then I got to thinking that I had to get up early, so I took off.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.000
Did you notice anything strange or unusual about the house or about the way the family acted?

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:22.080
There's always.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:29.890
When you're driving out of town, nothing out of the way in the street, the building, or the sky, no unusual light, some sound.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:36.840
movement heard something sound like thunder and about midnight a plane went

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:49.720
over all right dave i can call you again if i need you oh yes sure edna is that the telephone

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:56.540
company's official transcripts i put through the last call to ludlow at 11 59 p.m when did you

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.920
first notice anything wrong with the ludlow connection four

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.920
I phoned the company linesman to go out there right away.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.000
So the telephone lines could have gone down anywhere between 11.59 and 4.45.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.590
All right, Edna, thanks. You've been very helpful.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:20.030
Oh, Colonel Sturgeon. Miss Ames, National Wire Service.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:20.820
How do you do?

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:24.630
Audrey Ames? I've read a lot of your stuff, seen a lot of your photographs.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:27.280
Yes?

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:59.900
right there. I hope you understand our problem, our need to keep this quiet. Yes, captain's briefed me. If anyone wants me, I can be reached in Ludlow by radio. Yes, sir. How's chances of me going along? Not this trip, maybe later. In any case, not until we know what's out there. Oh, by the way, Colonel, my camera was taken from me at the roadblock by one of your men. I'll give orders to have it released. Oh, there has to be a logical explanation.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.280
The town of 150 people just doesn't disappear.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:05.850
This one did.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:29.960
I'd like to place a...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.960
Person-to-person call, please, to Mr. Norman Taggart,

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.830
Editor-in-Chief of National Wire Service.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:39.260
The number is Murray Hill 44836.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:40.840
In what city, please?

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:41.730
New York.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.500
Oh, you wind up that jet plane story already?

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:48.160
I'm not on the jet story.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.580
Norm, listen, I'm on to something I think can be real big.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:54.280
Where?

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:58.580
Oh, brother.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.360
Huh? What do you mean we can't print it?

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.130
I've given my word to hold off for a while.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:09.420
Now listen, Norm. A plane flew over Ludlow last night about midnight.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.620
Just about the time the lid blew off. Check on it.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:15.740
And, uh, check Washington. See if they had an atomic installation in the Ludlow area.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:17.020
Okay.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.400
Call me back as soon as you have anything, right? Goodbye.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.280
Sorry, miss. You can't. Oh, it's you again.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:42.200
I know I can't, but I'd like to have my camera back, please.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:44.180
Colonel Sturgeon said he was going to speak to somebody.

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:49.060
Hey, Corporal, Mathias, she wants her camera back.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:58.620
Hello, Norm, what's the score?

00:13:59.350 --> 00:13:59.870
Wrong track.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.720
The airlines confirm a commercial liner over Ludlow at 12.03 last night.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:10.760
There are no atomic installations, secret or otherwise, within 75 miles of Ludlow.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:12.220
Well, it was a possibility.

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:19.010
The only people who have been playing around with radioactive materials in your vicinity is the U.S. Department of Agriculture.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:20.940
Department of Agriculture?

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.920
Yeah, they've got an experimental project just outside Paxton.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.030
U.S. Department of Agriculture, Illinois Experimental Station.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.430
Got that. I'll keep in touch.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:40.060
I beg your pardon. I beg your pardon?

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:49.920
Hello.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:55.040
Oh, hello. I... I spoke to him, but I guess he didn't hear me.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:59.430
Oh, he's a deaf mute. Working with radiation can be dangerous.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.580
Accident last year cost him his speech and his hearing.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.330
I'm sorry. I'm looking for the project director.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.000
I'm the project director. My name's Ed Wainwright.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.560
What can I do for you? Oh, excuse me a minute.

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:23.340
Sorry, Frank. Nothing to worry about, just a slight catastrophe.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.450
Do you have these catastrophes very often?

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.970
All the time. Sorry to keep these little things from...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.280
getting in these are snails last summer it was caterpillars and after that it was grasshoppers

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:44.370
by the drove and just last week it was beetles now what can i do for you mr wainwright i'm audrey

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:49.740
ames i'm with national wire service i suppose you've heard about what happened in ludlow yes

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:55.730
i'm trying to find out what was responsible and it occurred to me that radiation of some

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:59.410
sort might have caused the destruction out there no i don't think so here

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:12.180
We're the only people around here using radioactive materials, and isotopes aren't explosive, so I'm afraid your answer isn't here.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:17.670
Oh, I see. Tell me, is this thing really a strawberry?

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:25.680
Yes, and these are tomatoes. This, we hope, is the future of the American farmer, and for that matter, all farmers everywhere.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:27.600
Can you eat them?

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.970
No, not yet, but we hope to develop...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.960
one day a hybrid that can be eaten how is it there hasn't been any publicity on this oh there

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.980
have been a few stories in farm journals but the most of the public these giants are just freaks

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:48.610
of nature no practical value well how do they get so big well radiation causes photosynthesis that

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:54.760
is the the growing process to continue night and day radioisotopes act as a sort of artificial sun

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:55.840
Son that never sets.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.300
It's fascinating.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.020
Tell me, what's he doing?

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.380
That's plant food, essential minerals.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:04.010
Keeps the plants from burning themselves out.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:05.230
They have to be fed constantly.

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:07.930
Actually, the fruit would grow much larger

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:09.330
if we didn't limit the stimulation.

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:12.890
Maybe you'd like to do a story on us.

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:14.510
I'd be glad to tell you more about the work.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.090
Thank you very much.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.630
I'd be very interested, but...

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.120
Well, right now, I'm working on this Ludlow story.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:21.950
Thanks again.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:24.360
All right. Bye.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:25.440
Bye.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:41.610
You here?

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:43.920
Well, he wouldn't let me inside, so...

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.770
Colonel, Brinkfield is on the line.

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:47.630
Colonel, you just got back from Ludlow.

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:49.110
You said after you got back, I could go.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:49.870
