00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:07.400
No, it couldn't have been.

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:10.080
Any explosion big enough to destroy this warehouse

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:11.460
would certainly have been heard in Ludlow.

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:14.380
What was it they kept stored here?

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.660
Wheat. Almost a million bushels.

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:18.420
That's a lot of wheat.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.640
And there was surplus to keep the market from being oversupplied.

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:24.040
Doesn't it strike you as a little strange

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:26.500
that out of all that wheat, there's not a grain left anywhere?

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:28.450
No, that's nature's way, Audrey.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:29.970
Birds probably cleaned up the leaves.

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:46.220
See what you mean, Frank?

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:47.320
What is it, Ed?

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:50.840
See how barren this ground is?

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.760
I don't know. I've seen horses leave it like this.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:58.820
This is deeper than horses go and much more thorough.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:01.240
Practically down to the roots.

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:02.780
The horses pick and choose.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:03.520
They leave patches.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:05.980
But this is completely barren.

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:13.940
Leave it to old Frank.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.280
He'll make a botanist out of me yet.

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:19.370
You're head of a government lab and you need lessons in botany?

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:22.760
Well, I try to teach him what I can about my field of study.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.200
He tries to teach me what he can about his.

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:26.530
Aren't you a botanist?

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:27.590
No, no.

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:28.490
I'm an entomologist.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:29.790
The study of insects.

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:32.540
Well, then how come you're working with plants?

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:37.930
Well, the existence and development of plants and insects are very closely related.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.730
They're highly dependent on one another.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:42.770
As a plain matter of fact, one couldn't live without the other.

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:44.960
And that's why I can't understand.

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:48.220
Ground like this is usually teeming with insects.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.560
This area is completely devoid of it.

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:54.060
I'd like to get some shots of this.

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:55.580
I'm going back to the car for my camera.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:56.160
All right.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:05.980
Here, let me help you.

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:10.070
What was that?

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:11.580
I don't know.

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:53.880
Geiger counters show no radioactivity to speak of, sir. Only background.

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:55.980
There's got to be an explanation somewhere.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.680
I've got your explanation for you, Tom.

00:27:57.980 --> 00:27:58.160
Ed.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:27:59.700
Now listen.

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:01.620
You've known me ever since I came to Paxton.

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:03.000
You know I'm not given to hysteria,

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:04.640
and you've got to listen to me with an open mind.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:05.600
Take it easy, Ed.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:06.780
Locusts.

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:08.350
What are you talking about?

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:10.090
I'm talking about giant locusts.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:12.350
Giant locusts are responsible for all of this.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:13.760
Are you nuts?

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.520
No evidence of any explosion, Colonel.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:18.210
Buildings look more like they were hit with a battering ram.

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.100
We found these guns at the scene,

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:22.200
the kind people keep in their homes,

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:23.360
and they've been fired.

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:24.460
Okay.

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:24.920
Lieutenant.

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:25.920
Now listen, Tom.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:28.120
These are eight feet tall, some even bigger.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:29.660
They're vicious, merciless killers.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:30.540
Oh, Ed.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:33.200
Lieutenant.

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:35.240
Lieutenant, phone Springfield again.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:36.800
Tell him I'm still waiting for those specialists.

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:37.420
Yes, sir.

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:38.860
Frank Johnson is dead.

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:40.360
He was killed not half an hour ago.

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:41.040
It was horrible.

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:43.800
Obviously, you're both under a strain, but...

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:44.260
Won't you listen?

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:47.420
You've got to get some soldiers out there before more people are killed.

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:50.990
Miss Ames, the governor asked me to exercise discretion in dealing with you.

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:52.790
Please don't make it any harder for me than it is.

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:53.790
You have to believe us.

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:56.690
Listen, you've seen the giant plants out at the lab.

00:28:57.720 --> 00:28:59.860
Are you trying to tell me you bred these things?

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:02.140
In a sense, I did, yes.

00:29:03.800 --> 00:29:05.400
Some locusts must have gotten into the lab,

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:08.520
and they ate some of the plants or some of the radioactive plant food.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.960
But their cell division accelerated immediately.

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:13.020
That is, they started to grow abnormally fast.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:16.480
They had to have a constant food supply to sustain this growth.

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:19.930
So, a couple of months ago, they wandered into the grain elevator outside of town.

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.610
When they grew to this giant size, they pushed their way out.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:24.680
Well, they just pushed the building down.

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:29.500
Yes. Each one of them has the strength of ten men.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.380
Probably two or three hundred of them.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:34.380
So, last night, not satisfied with eating the grain they came to Ludlow?

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:34.780
Yes.

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:39.410
Even if I went for your story about the size, it would be hard to believe they'd attack people.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:42.620
Sergeant, that report come in from the chemists?

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:43.560
No, not yet, sir.

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:44.580
Why won't you listen?

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:45.860
I am listening.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:48.920
We saw Frank Johnson killed by a giant locust.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:53.160
Sure, and there are reliable people who've also seen flying saucers and weird little men from Mars.

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:56.310
Well, take another look at that town out there, or have you found the answer.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:29:58.790
Lieutenant.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:00.650
Yes, sir.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:02.810
I'm taking a detail out for a look around.

00:30:03.220 --> 00:30:04.260
You're to keep radio contact.

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:05.120
I want ten men.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:05.960
Get them on the truck.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:06.480
Yes, sir.

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:07.880
All right, Ed.

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:11.500
You can show me the exact spot where you saw...

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:14.060
Well, whatever it was you saw.

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:14.540
No.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:17.410
Well, after all, it's his grasshopper.

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:05.390
All right. All right, man, let's go.

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:06.010
Eat as a rat.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:15.960
Man, we're gonna dig us some crazy grasshoppers.

00:31:16.330 --> 00:31:18.370
Why don't they give us nets instead of rifles?

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.040
All right, tell the people to spread out.

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:22.420
All right, man, pan out!

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:32.360
Here's the spot, Tom.

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:34.930
Where's the body?

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:37.500
There isn't any body.

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:39.570
Just like Ludlow.

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:42.460
All right, man.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:43.500
Into the woods.

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:59.970
Maybe we could train one of these giant wuzzits to pull a fly...

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.730
Chuck, I don't like it tight.

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:04.690
Take it easy.

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:11.680
That was a good eating.

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:14.180
Yeah, mustard and ketchup.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.660
No kidding, I ate them once, down in Mexico.

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:26.000
You better watch your step, they'll able to get even.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:37.640
They must have seen something. Mr. Wainwright's a scientist.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:38.880
He's trained to see things right.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:41.160
Well, these days they blame the atom for everything.

00:32:41.320 --> 00:32:43.660
Bad health, bad crops, bad weather.

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:45.340
Now it's grasshoppers.

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:47.850
They couldn't have just dreamed up this guy Frank being knocked off.

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:47.080
Also get some air support.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:48.540
We'll bomb them out of that forest.

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:50.670
What are you going to do?

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:53.140
Going in there and wipe out every last one.

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:53.920
They'll slaughter you.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:54.970
Not this time.

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:57.590
I want light artillery brought up with Company H.

00:34:57.860 --> 00:34:58.800
You don't have enough men.

00:34:59.500 --> 00:34:59.980
There'll be three...

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.400
Regiments out there tomorrow by oh 300 you don't understand you still don't have enough men not enough men for a couple hundred locusts

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:09.220
There are more than a couple hundred but you said before that was before I heard them screech the noise

00:35:09.220 --> 00:35:12.820
They made convinces me there are more. How many are there? I don't know there could be countless numbers

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:14.970
I think you better call in the regular army town

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:18.450
Where would I get off calling for the regular army to handle some oversized grasshoppers?

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:26.120
Why they'd sectionate me right out of the service jenna big charge of the East sector making a mistake Tom

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:36.010
I'm afraid he doesn't understand how serious this is.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.080
Well, after all, he knows what the military is capable of.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:40.590
Well, that's just it.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:43.360
He has faith in regimental firepower because he's seen at work

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:46.250
that he's never come up against an enemy like this before.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:47.710
And you've done all you can.

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:51.160
No, no, I haven't. In a way, I feel responsible, Audrey.

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:54.570
If he's in deadly danger if those locusts break out of the forest.

00:35:57.660 --> 00:35:58.280
What are you gonna do?

00:35:59.180 --> 00:35:59.880
I'm going to Washington.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.800
Maybe the army people will listen to me.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:04.700
I'll go with you. Maybe I can help. I saw them too.

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:07.860
All right. They've got to convince them, Audrey.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:10.390
We may be witnessing the beginning of an era

00:36:10.390 --> 00:36:12.290
that'll mean the complete annihilation of man.

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:14.000
Annihilation?

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:17.620
Annihilation. Beginning of the end.

00:36:30.760 --> 00:36:31.840
This gentleman is the enemy.

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:35.170
This locust, more commonly known as the grasshopper,

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:38.360
is almost identical to the giant locust of Ludlow,

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:42.020
except for its size and the fact that the giant's wings

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:43.700
fail to develop, they cannot fly.

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:47.940
The locust is intelligent, it's strong.

00:36:49.250 --> 00:36:50.250
Locusts follow a leader.

00:36:51.160 --> 00:36:52.300
Like the bee and the ant,

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:53.820
they're able to communicate with each other.

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:57.380
This communication or call is produced by the hind legs.

00:36:58.930 --> 00:36:59.970
This is the 19-

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:09.120
1856 Australian locust plague covered an area of 400 square miles. We've been plagued by locusts since biblical times

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:11.500
We've tried various forms of combating them

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:16.210
As a matter of fact in our own country the early settlers of the Massachusetts Bay Colony

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:25.350
Armed themselves with bundles of brush and drove millions of locusts into the sea. Now today despite the fact that we've developed

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:29.910
Powerful insecticides the locust still inflicts damages to the tomb

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:38.940
of 25 million dollars in the united states alone california colorado texas even this small locust

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:45.160
will attack a man it has two powerful jaws that are edged with sharp teeth it will kill other

00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:51.030
insects and devour them if no other insects are available it becomes a cannibal turning on its

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:59.960
own kind now you've seen what the locust can do in its normal size smaller than

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:03.710
Imagine, if you will, a locust that's grown bigger than a man

00:38:03.710 --> 00:38:05.930
and is continuing to grow, some larger than others,

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:07.150
but each one a deadly killer.

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:10.090
I hope you realize we haven't much time.

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:12.630
You are a scientist, Mr. Wainwright.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:14.000
You know what locusts can do.

00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:16.260
I'm a soldier. I know what guns can do.

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:19.350
I feel secure the Illinois National Guard

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:20.490
can handle this situation.

00:38:22.330 --> 00:38:24.130
Did you want to say something, General Hanson?

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:25.880
No, sir.

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:29.870
I was greatly impressed with your presentation, Wainwright.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:30.960
I'm sure all of us were.

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:32.540
Thank you very much for coming.

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:36.770
I'm afraid my presentation didn't impress you quite enough, General.

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:38.570
I don't understand you.

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:40.970
I mean that when the locusts start to move out of that forest,

00:38:41.090 --> 00:38:42.450
I'm not sure you'll be able to stop them.

00:38:42.940 --> 00:38:44.920
What are you suggesting we do, Mr. Wainwright?

00:38:45.320 --> 00:38:46.820
Hit them with everything you've got, now.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:48.580
You need more men, a lot more men.

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:49.980
You need tanks and heavy artillery.

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:52.940
As of now, the full strength of the Illinois National Guard

00:38:52.940 --> 00:38:55.220
is in the lines surrounding the Ludlow, Illinois forest.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:58.530
As I said before, Mr. Wainwright, you are a scientist.

00:38:59.210 --> 00:38:59.970
Why not leave the fire...

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:12.180
Urgent call from Paxton, Illinois, sir.

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:18.260
I'll show it here.

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:28.230
I see. Thank you.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:31.990
to a flight of Paxton with Mr. Wainwright.

00:39:32.350 --> 00:39:33.330
We'll take charge of operations.

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:37.340
Wainwright, I owe you an apology.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:39.840
The locusts have broken through our defense lines.

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:41.140
Thousands of passengers.

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.780
Our troops are reorganizing to prevent Paxton itself

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:45.660
from being overrun.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:54.450
The held position in the suburbs of Paxton must be held.

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:58.790
Otherwise, the 100 miles between Paxton and Chicago will lie open.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.120
We can't land in Paxton, sir.

00:40:03.190 --> 00:40:03.670
Why not?

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:05.710
It just came over the radio, sir.

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:09.040
Giant locusts have overrun Paxton at 1,400.

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:15.140
Head for Chicago.

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:16.150
Yes, sir.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:36.120
Captain, I want these positions held. I want them held at all costs, if humanly possible.

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:39.700
Captain, have your map people immediately turn out 300 overlays.

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:42.720
Showing the Chicago defense line is tentatively set.

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:44.500
Yes.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.070
Major Everett, what about Squadron 12?

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:04.550
They haven't reported any yet, General.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:05.750
Well, let me know the moment you hear.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:06.190
Yes, sir.

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:08.040
I'll be with you in a moment.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:09.200
Bill!

00:41:09.850 --> 00:41:12.990
You'll proceed at once to temporary GHQ, Central Sector, and assume command.

00:41:13.730 --> 00:41:18.470
Norm, you will order the 81st Armored Division to proceed without delay from Fort Sheridan to Chicago.

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:20.230
Have the CG report to me upon arrival.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:23.200
Request the Defense Department to alert the following divisions.

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:29.240
The 1st Armored, the 40th, the 1st Airborne, the 92nd, and the 76th.

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:31.480
Now, what's your problem?

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:32.240
Food.

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:35.050
At the rate refugees are pouring into Chicago General,

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:37.010
our emergency stocks won't last long.

00:41:46.760 --> 00:41:48.080
Toxathene is one we haven't tried.

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:49.160
How good is it?

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:50.950
Almost as powerful as Chlordane.

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:54.020
All right, ship 2,000 gallons out to the base.

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:55.760
Excuse me.

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:01.420
They're speaking.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:03.810
Quadrant 12 just reported.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:06.280
They say they came down on the deck

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:09.490
and practically drowned the enemy in insecticide.

00:42:10.910 --> 00:42:12.430
Chlordane didn't even slow them down.

00:42:12.770 --> 00:42:15.490
You'll have to come up with something stronger.

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:17.760
I'll do my best, General.

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:19.210
Goodbye.

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:22.960
Stuff's no good.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:23.880
Forget it.

00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:42.290
It's pretty bad, doesn't it?

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:44.310
I thought you went back to New York.

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:45.880
Well, the big story's here.

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:48.180
Well, look, your editor called you back

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:49.240
because it was too dangerous.

00:42:49.760 --> 00:42:50.740
I wanted to be here.

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:55.780
Nothing we've done so far.

00:42:56.860 --> 00:42:59.710
Insecticides, fire, bombs, nothing has done any good.

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:01.610
There's so little time.

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:03.880
Well, you're doing everything you can.

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:05.950
Well, everybody is, but it's not good enough.

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:11.380
Time will come when the beasts will inherit the earth.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:31.240
There's no sign of the locust yet, General.

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:32.240
Out.

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:44.990
With an arc of five divisions to the west and south of Chicago,

00:43:45.720 --> 00:43:48.380
General Hanson is confident that he can keep the menace at bay.

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:51.800
Units of the Air Forces and Marines are moving into position

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:54.950
to back up those forces already deployed by the Army.

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:59.960
The one advantage our forces hold over the enemy is that they always...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.840
reveal their intention to attack.

00:44:02.748 --> 00:44:03.888
Before every attack,

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:08.300
The locust send forth this warning in the form of a high-pitched screech.

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:11.020
Now, this screech increases in intensity.

00:44:11.516 --> 00:44:13.836
until it reaches ear-shattering proportions.

00:44:14.716 --> 00:44:17.556
And it's when this screech reaches its full intensity...

00:44:17.852 --> 00:44:18.912
that the locusts attack.

00:44:22.908 --> 00:44:24.908
We've got every available man on the line, Major,

00:44:24.908 --> 00:44:26.908
and I think there's no question but what will be.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.000
This program for an important announcement

00:47:02.556 --> 00:47:06.696
The giant locusts have reached the Chicago South Side and nearby suburbs.

00:47:06.816 --> 00:47:07.276
I repeat.

00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:11.600
The giant locusts have reached the Chicago south side and nearby suburbs.

00:47:12.316 --> 00:47:13.156
Keep calm.

00:47:13.564 --> 00:47:15.164
Take shelter in the basement.

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:17.660
a shelter in basements.

00:47:18.268 --> 00:47:19.628
Do not panic.

00:47:20.316 --> 00:47:21.356
Attention, please.

00:47:21.852 --> 00:47:26.492
Attention, please. Keep moving. Do not block the highway.

00:47:27.132 --> 00:47:29.552
Push stalled cars off-road at once.

00:47:30.604 --> 00:47:30.864
Peace.

00:47:30.896 --> 00:47:32.196
All traffic moving.

00:47:33.036 --> 00:47:33.956
Do not panic.

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:29.980
I sent for you because of a new development that may be...

00:48:31.390 --> 00:48:32.950
The locusts have stopped their advance.

00:48:33.600 --> 00:48:36.200
They're huddled in the alleys and buildings just outside the loop.

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:37.490
The locusts got cold.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:41.490
When the temperature drops below 68 degrees, they just stop moving.

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:44.080
Well, maybe now's the time we could move in and destroy them.

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:45.860
Well, they aren't in hibernation, sir.

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:48.700
They'll move if they're provoked, and they're just as deadly as ever.

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:52.060
When the sun comes out tomorrow, they'll be active again.

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:54.220
The Air Force is standing by.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:56.620
The B-52 loaded with an atom bomb.

00:48:59.330 --> 00:48:59.970
Drop an atom bomb.

00:49:00.090 --> 00:49:00.950
A bomb on Chicago?

00:49:02.200 --> 00:49:04.260
Washington has given me authority to do just that,

00:49:04.580 --> 00:49:05.800
as a last contingency.

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:08.020
If the bomb is dropped early tomorrow,

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:09.140
there'll be no loss of life.

00:49:09.870 --> 00:49:11.510
The city will be evacuated by then.

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:13.120
But what about the property?

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:15.380
There'll be a billion dollars worth of damage

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:17.460
in a site that's too contaminated to rebuild on.

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:18.780
I realize that.

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:20.990
But if we don't drop the bomb,

00:49:21.130 --> 00:49:22.530
Chicago will almost certainly fall.

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:26.400
Bomber crew is alerted for a drop at dawn.

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:29.620
If we don't come up with something by then...

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:33.540
I'll make a final check with Washington and relay their okay.

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:40.170
Isn't there a chance the locusts could die in the night of the cold?

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:42.600
No, not at this time of year.

00:49:43.640 --> 00:49:46.240
It takes 24 hours of exposure at 14 degrees.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:51.060
Isn't there some way you could drive them into the lake?

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:53.100
In Washington, you said the settlers did that.

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:54.380
Settlers?

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:55.330
Hmm?

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:59.670
At least, settlers in Massachusetts did.

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:03.500
Literally drive them into the sea, but they weren't dealing with giants.

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:10.550
Wait a minute. Can't drive them.

00:50:12.070 --> 00:50:15.880
Drive. Could attract them.

00:50:16.660 --> 00:50:17.020
Attract.

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:21.130
If we could reproduce their call, General, it might work. It just might work.

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:23.160
A call for insects?

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:25.950
Have you ever been duck hunting, General?

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:27.470
The duck call?

00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:29.740
Yes. There's one for bees, too.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:31.940
Use it in apiaries. It might work.

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:33.240
What do you need?

00:50:34.150 --> 00:50:37.980
Well, let me see. I need, uh, I need an audio oscillator.

00:50:38.520 --> 00:50:41.120
I need an audio two. Two audio amplifiers.

00:50:41.180 --> 00:50:43.020
The most powerful you can get. I need an oscilloscope.

00:50:43.480 --> 00:50:45.220
I need some high-frequency radio equipment.

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:47.710
And a boat. Fast boat.

00:50:48.850 --> 00:50:49.970
Whatever you need, we'll get.

00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:52.890
It's one thing I have to get myself.

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:54.820
Something that'll tell me when I've succeeded.

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:55.730
What's that?

00:50:56.480 --> 00:50:57.780
A live giant grasshopper.

00:51:38.960 --> 00:51:48.240
I suggest that you send some of them that way and some down there, and you and I try the alley.

00:51:48.640 --> 00:51:51.980
Okay. You four men go that way. You men that way.

00:51:54.130 --> 00:51:54.410
Go.

00:54:00.190 --> 00:54:24.130
Okay, we got one of the smaller grasshoppers.

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:29.460
Too close, Major.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:32.440
You put that cage up in a hurry, and I don't know how strong it is.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:36.090
A month ago, I was teaching my engineering class at the university.

00:54:36.910 --> 00:54:38.070
It was safe and secure.

00:54:38.350 --> 00:54:39.760
And look at me now.

00:54:39.940 --> 00:54:46.800
You know, I'm 37 years old,

00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:48.990
and all of a sudden I realize for the first time

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:50.430
how much I've taken life for granted.

00:54:51.540 --> 00:54:53.460
Guess that's something you can't take for granted, Major.

00:54:54.520 --> 00:54:56.520
Ed, how will you know when you've got the right sound?

00:54:57.470 --> 00:54:58.090
He'll tell us?

00:54:58.330 --> 00:54:58.630
How?

00:54:59.470 --> 00:54:59.970
He'll react.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:06.280
to it and this polygraph will record his reaction how long will it take it's a matter of trial and

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:20.580
error it'll take 10 minutes or 10 hours we don't have 10 hours dropping that bomb at dawn how does

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:26.960
this work well i've just attached these wires from the polygraph to the two copper strips at

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:29.820
the base of the cage now the locusts like every other

00:55:30.280 --> 00:55:35.760
thing has galvanic reflexes or electrical charges and direct ratio to its activity or emotional

00:55:35.760 --> 00:55:41.810
stimulation i don't understand well in other words when we hit the correct sound or signal

00:55:41.810 --> 00:55:47.380
the grasshopper will react to it and the polygraph will record the change now you notice how steady

00:55:47.380 --> 00:55:52.900
and regular the movement of the needle is now but when we reproduce the grasshopper's call the lines

00:55:52.900 --> 00:55:58.160
will become longer and highly irregular it's like a lie detector test isn't it uh-huh but it's the

00:55:58.160 --> 00:55:59.600
First time a grasshopper ever got one.

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:04.520
Now, if you'll keep your eye on the needle for any unusual jumps or dips, we'll get to work.

00:56:32.810 --> 00:56:34.030
When right, you've had your chance.

00:56:34.330 --> 00:56:35.650
Now it's the Air Force's turn.

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:40.930
Operator.

00:56:42.850 --> 00:56:44.610
Operator, this is a top priority call.

00:56:45.520 --> 00:56:47.020
Get me General Wagner at the air base.

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:04.970
Look, General, the locusts outside will stay put until the temperature reaches 68 degrees.

00:57:05.190 --> 00:57:07.210
That's about an hour and a half from now.

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:14.410
Wagner, cargo has been evacuated.

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:19.070
Unless you receive instructions to the contrary by 0616,

00:57:19.710 --> 00:57:22.850
that is, that is 90 minutes from now,

00:57:24.460 --> 00:57:27.700
you will order your B-52 crew to deliver the bomb on the designated target.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:41.060
the instructions back to me correct there's no time to fool with that now major get a detail

00:57:41.060 --> 00:57:45.590
of men up here to move this stuff bring some extra grenades they're selling my staff car

00:57:45.590 --> 00:57:52.250
new lab set up for you my new hq outside the city i can't move now well you can't stay here not

00:57:52.250 --> 00:57:56.520
with an atom bomb hanging over your head i don't have any choice look up to now we've

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:59.890
been using a filtered signal and it hasn't worked but it's just possible

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:01.660
that the hearing apparatus of the locus

00:58:01.660 --> 00:58:04.320
can detect harmonic frequencies above the human range.

00:58:04.460 --> 00:58:06.880
Well, to test these frequencies, I need every minute that's left.

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:09.080
If you'll just give me one man to replace Miss Ames.

00:58:09.120 --> 00:58:09.520
I'm staying.

00:58:11.380 --> 00:58:13.840
Well, look, this is no time to be worrying about a big story.

00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:15.260
I'm not worried about a story.

00:58:16.470 --> 00:58:18.010
Will you please leave before it's too late?

00:58:19.220 --> 00:58:19.540
No.

00:58:20.300 --> 00:58:23.790
Major, you want a detail of two men will remain here with Mr. Wainwright.

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:26.620
I will station men at three observation posts.

00:58:27.190 --> 00:58:29.980
One in the suburbs, one near the Ardenst...

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:32.340
and one on top of a downtown hotel.

00:58:33.040 --> 00:58:35.180
Also, a helicopter will spot from above.

00:58:35.920 --> 00:58:38.820
There will be a getaway car parked downstairs at the main entrance.

00:58:39.400 --> 00:58:43.670
I suggest you use it by 5.45 at the very latest.

00:58:43.930 --> 00:58:49.130
If you are successful, contact me at once so I can stop the bomb.

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:52.000
Got it.

01:01:37.480 --> 01:01:38.900
Emergency lab to Chicago GHQ.

01:01:39.080 --> 01:01:40.820
Emergency lab to Chicago GHQ.

01:01:40.920 --> 01:01:41.740
Come in, General. Are you there?

01:01:41.940 --> 01:01:43.790
Now listen to me, Wayne Wright.

01:01:44.230 --> 01:01:44.970
We found it, General.

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:46.220
We found the frequency.

01:01:46.700 --> 01:01:48.970
Good. Now stand by.

01:01:49.820 --> 01:01:51.220
I've got to stop that A-bomb.

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:53.100
I'll get right back to you.

01:01:54.120 --> 01:01:55.460
Chicago GHQ to double B.

01:01:56.020 --> 01:01:57.380
Chicago GHQ to double B.

01:02:00.890 --> 01:02:03.240
Chicago GHQ to Double B. Over.

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:08.200
This is Double B. Come in, Chicago GHQ. Over.

01:02:09.010 --> 01:02:13.950
Hanson here. Your mission is canceled. Return to your base.

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:17.430
Keep the message. Over.

01:02:19.150 --> 01:02:21.840
Mission canceled. Return to base. Over.

01:02:22.400 --> 01:02:24.200
Chicago GHQ to Emergency Lab.

01:02:25.030 --> 01:02:25.470
Right here.

01:02:26.620 --> 01:02:28.120
The show is yours, Wainwright.

01:02:29.320 --> 01:02:29.620
All right.

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:32.880
Get that equipment on the lake in a boat, and I'll be your Pied Piper.

01:02:33.020 --> 01:02:53.740
